.he "sh (1)                       8/13/26                       sh (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
sh
- the micronix shell
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
sh [ -v ] [ -c string ] [ file ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Sh reads lines and runs them.  With a file it reads that; with -c it
runs the one string it is given and stops; with neither it reads the
standard input, and prompts if that is a terminal.
.sp
A line is one or more statements separated by semicolons.  A statement
is a pipeline: commands separated by a pipe, with redirections and
perhaps an ampersand on the end.  Each command is looked up as an
alias first, then as a builtin, then along the path, which is the
current directory, /bin and /usr/bin.
.sp
This is a csh rather than a Bourne shell.  The operators ending in an
ampersand send the standard error wherever the standard output is
going, and there is no here document and no way to redirect a numbered
descriptor.
.sp
A shell whose name begins with a minus is a login shell.  It reads
/.login and then /.sh before it reads anything else.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-v	Write each line to the standard error as it is run.

.ti -10
-c	Run the string that follows, and exit.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPERATORS
.in +5
.br
Longest first, which is the order they are matched in:
.sp
.nf
     |&    pipe the standard output and the error
     |     pipe the standard output
     &     run the pipeline in the background
     >>&   append both to a file
     >>    append the standard output to a file
     >&    both to a file
     >     the standard output to a file
     <     the standard input from a file
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
QUOTING AND SUBSTITUTION
.in +5
.br
A double quote takes what is inside it as one word, and ends the word
before it: /etc/"pass"* is the three words /etc/, pass, and the
expansion of *.  What is quoted is taken as it stands - no pattern in
it is expanded and no command in it is run.  There is no single
quote: 'x' is the three characters that were typed.  A backslash
protects the character after it from the shell, but not from pattern
matching.
.sp
A command between backquotes is run and what it writes takes its
place, split into words.  The backquote ends a word rather than
joining one, so
.sp
.nf
     echo A`echo hi`B
.fi
.sp
is the three words A, hi and B.  Backquotes inside double quotes are
not run.
.sp
Commands inside parentheses run in a shell of their own, so what they
change they change only for themselves:
.sp
.nf
     (cd /etc ; pwd) ; pwd
.fi
.sp
writes /etc and then the directory you were in.  A group may be
redirected, piped or backgrounded like any other command, and may
hold anything a line may hold, groups included.  A group and ordinary
words cannot be mixed in one command.
.sp
An unterminated quote, backquote or parenthesis is an error and the
line is abandoned.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PATTERNS
.in +5
.br
A word holding one of these is replaced by the files it matches,
sorted:
.sp
.nf
     *      any run of characters, including none
     ?      one character
     [...]  one of these; a-z is a range
     [^...] one character that is not one of these
.fi
.sp
A pattern may appear in any part of a path, so /e*/passwd finds it.
A leading dot is matched like any other character, though . and ..
are never matched.
.sp
A pattern that matches nothing is left out.  Only when nothing in the
whole command matched is it an error, and then the shell writes "No
match." and runs no part of that command.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUILTINS
.in +5
.br
.nf
     cd, chdir  change directory
     home       what cd with no argument means
     echo       write the arguments
     type       print the named files
     path       set or show the search path
     prompt     set the prompt
     alias      make or show an abbreviation
     unalias    take one away
     source     read a file as input
     nice       run the rest more politely
     kill       signal a process
     wait       wait for the background ones
     pid        the process id
     exit       exit, with a status if given
.fi
.sp
What is here needs the shell itself: cd moves it, exit ends it, source
changes where it reads from, alias and path and prompt and home are
its own tables, wait and pid are about its children and itself, and
nice sets what its children inherit.  Anything that does not is a
command.
.sp
dir and era were builtins and are now aliases, seeded before ~/.login
and ~/.sh are read:
.sp
.nf
     dir        ls
     era        rm
.fi
.sp
They are cp/m spellings rather than shell work, and as aliases they
can be seen with alias and changed with it.  sync was a builtin and is
now sync (1).
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PARAMETERS
.in +5
.br
In a script, $0 is the script and $1 through $9 are the arguments
after it; $* is all of them.  Only one digit is taken, so $10 is the
first argument followed by a zero.
.sp
There are no other variables.  $ before anything else, or before
nothing, is a $; a backslash protects it, and a $ inside double
quotes is not replaced.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
HISTORY
.in +5
.br
Only when the input is a terminal, and only in one form:
.sp
.nf
     !word	the last line beginning with word
.fi
.sp
The word runs to the next space, and the reference may be anywhere in
the line, so "echo !e" runs echo followed by the last line beginning
with e.  What is remembered is the line after it was expanded.  A line
with a ! in it is written out as it will be run.
.sp
There are no event numbers: !1 looks for a line beginning with 1.  If
there is none, the shell writes "Event not found." and runs nothing.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
.nf
     /.login    read by a login shell, first
     /.sh       read after /.login
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
There is no source for the shell this tree ships in /bin, so this one
was read back out of that binary: the operator table, the builtin
numbering, the search path, the prompt and the whole of the quoting
and substitution above are all its own, most of it settled by running
the image beside this one.  Where the
two can be told apart, cmd/sh/NOTES says so.
.sp
It installs as /bin/sh.  It did not for a while - the reconstruction
was too unfinished to be the shell a system comes up under, and it
went in as nsh so that a machine that would not start under it still
started.  The operators, the search path and the builtins all work
now, so it is the shell.
.sp
The one it replaces is a Whitesmiths binary with no source.  Keep a
copy of it: this is read back out of that binary and is not finished,
and the parts of it that are guesses are named in cmd/sh/NOTES.
.sp
The parts of it that are guesses are named in cmd/sh/NOTES, and
cmd/sh/regress.sh runs the two side by side.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sync (1), msh (1)
